<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alain Bouchart</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1530</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alain Bouchart was a Breton lawyer and chronicler who served Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> and Duchess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a>. His Grandes Croniques de Bretaigne, printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1514, told the history of Brittany from its legendary origins and presented it as an ancient kingdom equal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Widely read, it shaped the Breton sense of a separate past.</p></div>
